    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the number of different starting lineups possible, given that two specific players, Ace and Zeppo, must be included, we need to break down the problem as follows:\n\n1. Choose the 2 All- star players first.\n\nThere are 2) possible choices for Ace, and then the remaining player is Zeppo. So, the total number of combinations for the starting lineup is now 2).\n\n2. After selecting Ace and Zeppo, we need to choose the remaining 3 players from the 10 remaining players (since Ace and Zeppo are already picked) to complete the lineup.\n\nUsing combinations, the number of ways to select additional players now can be calculated by:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{3}\n\\]\n\n3. Multiply the number of ways to choose the 2 All- star players by the number of ways to choose the remaining 3 players:\n\n\\[\n2 \\times \\binom{10}{3} = 2 \\times \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1}\n\\]\n\n4. Simplify the fraction to find:\n\n\\[\n2 \\times 120 = 240\n\\]\n\nHence, the total number of different starting lineups possible is \\boxed{240}.",
